<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Viscounty of Béarn</h1><p class="st-meta">820 CE – 1620 CE (approx.) · capital Pau</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Pyrenean lordship that made itself a sovereign state under Gaston Fébus and gave France its king Henry IV.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Béarn emerged in the 9th century as a viscounty within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-gascony">Duchy of Gascony</a>, ruled by a family later called the Centulles from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lescar">Lescar</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morlaas">Morlaàs</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaston-iv-of-bearn">Gaston IV</a> took part in the First Crusade and helped Aragon conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> in 1118. The seat later moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orthez">Orthez</a> and, from 1464, to Pau.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fors-de-bearn">Fors de Béarn</a> guaranteed local liberties, and the viscount had to swear to respect them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaston-iii-of-foix">Gaston III</a>, called Fébus, refused homage to both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1347 and ruled Béarn as a sovereign land; he also wrote a famous treatise on hunting. Through marriages the viscounts became counts of Foix and kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeanne-dalbret">Queen Jeanne d'Albret</a> made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a> the official religion in the 1560s. Her son Henry, born at Pau, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iv-of-france">Henry IV of France</a> in 1589, but Béarn stayed separate until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiii-of-france">Louis XIII</a> annexed it in 1620 and restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/viscounty-of-bearn.jpg" alt="Viscounty of Béarn" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Patricia.fidi · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_B%C3%A9arn.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>820 CE – 1620 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pau</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gaston IV, Gaston Fébus, Jeanne d'Albret</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity, later Calvinism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Béarnese Occitan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Béarn, Sovereign Béarn</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1220&amp;focus=viscounty-of-bearn" aria-label="Viscounty of Béarn on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4671 641 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Viscounty of Béarn lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4991 897L5039 908M4991 897L4986 849M4991 897L4960 913M4991 897L5054 728M4991 897L4970 902"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5039 908h0M4986 849h0M4960 913h0M5054 728h0M4970 902h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4991 897h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4991 897h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1220&amp;focus=viscounty-of-bearn">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
